<!-- Aura-SOC - rule 100824 (level 5). Domain: local,npm-proxy,
     Naming convention and load-order trap: see rules/README.md -->
<group name="local,npm-proxy,">

  <!-- Web attack signature seen on the reverse proxy, answered with a CLIENT
       ERROR (4xx). Demotes 100700/100701/100702 from level 12/10 down to 5 for
       this case only.

       Why: nginx-proxy-manager fronts the whole lab on the public internet, so
       its access log is a continuous stream of untargeted scanning. Measured
       over 24 h: 243 436 events, of which the only level-12 alert was
       `GET //adminer.php -> 404` from 213.209.159.223 — a scanner probing for a
       database admin panel that does not exist. Feeding that to the LLM as
       HIGH/CRITICAL costs an API call and an IRIS case for an event where
       nothing was served.

       Scoped on the STATUS, not on the decoder alone, and this is the whole
       point: a 4xx means the server refused or could not find the resource, so
       the attack did not land. The same signature answered 200 or 302 keeps its
       original level and still reaches the AI — a web shell that actually
       responds is precisely what this proxy is worth watching for.

       `<id>` and not `<status>`: the npm-access decoder stores the HTTP status
       in the web-log field `id` (`status` is a reserved static Wazuh field name
       and fails at load time). See decoders/npm-proxy.xml.

       `<decoded_as>` restricts the demotion to the reverse proxy: the same
       parent rules also fire on the agents' own web logs, where a 404 carries a
       different meaning (the request already reached the host). -->

  <rule id="100824" level="5">
    <if_sid>100700,100701,100702</if_sid>
    <decoded_as>npm-access</decoded_as>
    <id>^4</id>
    <description>Reverse proxy: web attack signature answered with a client error (scan noise): $(url)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1595</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>web_scan,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
